  "fields": {
    "title": "Flat Design",
    "textContent": {
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Description",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "heading-3"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "The term flat design refers to a graphically minimalist design style. The focus lies on the colourfulness of the design. Flat design avoids the use of textures, ornaments, drop shadows and three-dimensional elements. The design is bound by stylistic restrictions and is reduced to essential elements. Flat Design is formally close to the design of European Modernism.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Through its reduced visual language, Flat Design questions the necessity of reproducing real objects and functions. Flat design is therefore seen as a counter-position to ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2Ib0yeXE5iLTu4gX6TDg0e",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [],
                  "value": "skeuomorphism,",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": " in which, for example, textures of different materialities and colour gradients are used to imitate real interactions in interface design.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "target": {
              "sys": {
                "id": "2ptFRbXnDoJ8YALGlGwwSN",
                "type": "Link",
                "linkType": "Asset"
              }
            }
          },
          "content": [],
          "nodeType": "embedded-asset-block"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "value": "Skeumorphism (left), Flat design (middle) and Neomorphism (right) in comparison",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Today, a further development of flat design can be seen, which is called ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "value": "semi-flat design",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": " or ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "value": "flat design 2.0",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": ". This design style is mostly flat, but uses subtle shadows, highlights and layers to add depth to the user interface and ensure better ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "6UzOyP2ueyoyoD8IMtYciu",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [],
                  "value": "usability",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": ". Google's ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "uri": "https://m3.material.io/"
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [],
                  "value": "material design",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": " language is an example of Flat 2.0: it uses consistent metaphors and principles of physics to help users understand user interfaces and interpret visual hierarchies.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Background",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "heading-3"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Although Flat Design was already implemented in Windows Phone 7 in 2010, it was not noticed as an major trend until 2012 with the release of Windows 8. Especially when Apple also switched most elements of its operating system to flat design with iOS 7 in 2013, it became super popular in ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2RmvRuimRiyYsVEo5kIhx0",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [],
                  "value": "webdesign",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": ". But there was also criticism about flat design: some UI experts feared that many users would find it more difficult to recognise certain functions that were previously considered as learned and the lack of ",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "7wUlGYvEjCDA8djn7fUJre",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [],
                  "value": "spatiality",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": " would make it harder to find your way around in an interface.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        }
      ],
      "nodeType": "document"
    },
